## The Determination Time Gap
★ [sit-316] The Determination Time Gap: A situation concept that describes the temporal mismatch between when a model's risk profile is measurable and when regulatory interventions are most effective.
Encompasses: Pre-training estimates, post-deployment impact assessment, and compliance preparation.
  Your interpretation (BDI breakdown):
    Belief: Reliable, actionable risk metrics for AI models are empirically unavailable until after deployment, meaning pre-deployment regulatory triggers would be based on speculative or incomplete data.
    Desire: Regulation ought to be deferred to late-stage, post-deployment oversight where actual evidence of harm exists, rather than imposing premature constraints on development.
    Intention: Advocate for flexible, adaptive regulatory frameworks that activate only once measurable post-deployment impact data is available, resisting mandatory pre-market approval gates.
  Safetyist:
    Belief: The gap between when a model's risk profile becomes measurable and when it is deployed means that by the time harms are observable, regulatory intervention is already too late to prevent them.
    Desire: Mandatory pre-market testing and early-stage reporting requirements ought to be established so that risk visibility is achieved before a model reaches users.
    Intention: Push for institutionalized pre-deployment evaluation protocols and structured disclosure obligations that close the determination time gap before products enter the market.
  Skeptic:
    Belief: Developers have historically exploited temporal ambiguity in measurement and reporting timelines to delay regulatory scrutiny until their products are already entrenched in the market.
    Desire: Accountability mechanisms ought to be designed to prevent developers from strategically timing disclosures in ways that circumvent regulatory review.
    Intention: Scrutinize industry self-reporting timelines for evidence of deliberate delay, and advocate for independent third-party audits with fixed, externally imposed measurement deadlines.

## Slow Thinking in Law-Making
★ [sit-314] Slow Thinking in Law-Making: A situation concept that advocates for deliberate, non-reactive legislative processes in the face of rapid technological change.
Encompasses: Resisting pressure for fast-paced regulation, applying Kahneman's slow thinking to policy, avoiding rash political approvals.
  Your interpretation (BDI breakdown):
    Belief: Prolonged legislative deliberation empirically slows the deployment cycle of AI technologies, causing measurable delays in the realization of economic and social benefits that rapid iteration would otherwise deliver.
    Desire: Legislative processes ought to be streamlined and minimally obstructive so that innovation can proceed at the pace dictated by technological possibility rather than political caution.
    Intention: We will argue against open-ended deliberative timelines by highlighting opportunity costs and lobbying for sunset clauses or regulatory sandboxes that allow deployment to continue while policy catches up.
  Safetyist:
    Belief: Complex, high-stakes AI systems have historically been integrated into society before their failure modes and systemic risks are adequately understood, and rushed legislation has repeatedly failed to anticipate catastrophic edge cases.
    Desire: Regulation of AI ought to be thorough and evidence-based, prioritizing comprehensive risk assessment over speed so that irreversible harms are prevented before deployment becomes entrenched.
    Intention: We will actively support deliberative mechanisms — expert panels, staged review processes, and mandatory impact assessments — that institutionalize slow thinking as a procedural norm in AI governance.
  Skeptic:
    Belief: Current legislative cycles around AI are dominated by industry-aligned actors who exploit urgency narratives to push through performative, superficial rules that entrench existing power imbalances and leave accountability gaps intact.
    Desire: Law-making ought to be genuinely deliberative in ways that center affected communities, labor interests, and marginalized groups — not merely slow for the sake of appearing rigorous while serving incumbent interests.
    Intention: We will use slow-thinking frameworks as a lever to demand participatory, transparent processes that expose safety-washing and force legislators to confront structural questions of power, bias, and corporate accountability.

